-- Passage rg6-theme-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('825b3092-fb82-5c21-bd93-b8164252b46a', 'rg6-theme-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Firefly and the Moon$q$, $q$One summer night, a young beetle wandered too far from his log and lost his way in the tall meadow grass. He called out for help, and his small voice drifted over the stems.

A firefly named Pip heard him. She flew to the top of a clover and looked around. The meadow was dark, and the grass was thick.

"Someone should help that beetle," Pip said to a cricket nearby. "The moon will rise soon. Its light will show him the way home."

So Pip waited. The cricket waited. A snail on a leaf waited too. They all watched the sky.

But clouds rolled in, thick and gray, and the moon stayed hidden behind them. The beetle's calls grew softer and more frightened.

Pip looked at her own tail. Her glow was tiny, no brighter than a grain of sugar in sunlight. "What good is a light like mine," she thought, "when even the moon cannot help?"

Still, the beetle sounded so alone. Pip flew down into the grass and flashed her light once, then again. The beetle stopped calling. "I see you!" he cried, and he crawled toward her.

Other fireflies noticed Pip blinking low in the grass. One by one, they drifted down beside her, until a little path of lights stretched across the meadow. The beetle followed it all the way back to his log.

Later, the clouds parted and the moon finally appeared. By then, everyone in the meadow was already asleep.$q$, 'draft')
