  ('8280ed7b-e75d-5f5c-a383-c4ced44f39c3', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-word-parts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The prefix com- means "together" or "with," and the Latin root pet means "seek." Why must a seed that lands under its parent "compete with that parent for light"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both plants seek the same limited light, so they struggle for it","is_correct":true},{"text":"The young seed blocks the light and makes the parent grow faster","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"},{"text":"Both plants share light equally, so neither one comes up short","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"The parent plant gives its light to the young seed as a gift","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Both plants seek the same limited light, so they struggle for it$q$, $q$Compete means "seek together," so two plants in the same spot are both trying to get the same light, which is why the passage says a traveling seed has a better chance. Sharing equally ignores the problem the passage describes.$q$, 'draft', 'ddd97707-5194-5811-90cf-ed551eae73fa')
