   $q$The prefix tri- means "three." Why could a "tricycle" stand steady on its own?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It is built very low, so its pedals touch the ground","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It has two wheels of equal size, like a safety bicycle","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-prefix-meaning"},{"text":"It has one large wheel that keeps it balanced","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-similar-word"},{"text":"It has three wheels, so it does not tip over when it stops","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It has three wheels, so it does not tip over when it stops$q$, $q$Tri means "three," so a tricycle has three wheels, which lets it stand by itself for young children. Two equal wheels describe a bicycle, which tips over unless the rider holds it up.$q$, 'draft', '98c91e10-4d4f-529e-8c24-b36cde61f489')
