values ('44f71698-e636-585c-aa57-1581e5df4808', 'rg7-argument-evidence-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Turn Lunch Scraps into Garden Soil$q$, $q$Every day, the cafeteria at Redfern Middle School throws away about 40 pounds of food scraps. Banana peels, apple cores and leftover salad go straight into the trash and then to the landfill. Redfern should start a composting program so those scraps can become soil for the school garden.

First, composting would cut the amount of trash the school sends away. Last spring, the seventh-grade science classes sorted one week of cafeteria waste. They found that food scraps made up nearly half of the total weight. Removing those scraps would mean fewer trash pickups.

Second, the school garden needs better soil. Ms. Okafor, who runs the garden club, says the beds are hard and dry by midsummer. Compost holds water and adds nutrients that help plants grow. Linwood Middle School, two towns away, began composting three years ago. It now grows enough lettuce to serve in its own salad bar.

Third, a compost program would teach science in a hands-on way. Students could measure the temperature inside the pile, track how fast scraps break down and test the finished soil.

The garden club also won a ribbon at the county fair last year for its sunflowers, which grew taller than any other entry.

Some people worry that compost bins will smell and attract pests. A well-run bin, however, is covered and turned each week, and it holds only fruit and vegetable scraps. Linwood reports no pest problems since it started.

Composting is a small step that turns waste into something useful. Redfern should begin this fall.$q$, 'draft')
