-- Passage rg7-argument-evidence-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('74bc284e-e718-5160-847f-499e25e1c7d6', 'rg7-argument-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Phones Belong in Lockers$q$, $q$At Westbrook Middle School, students may keep their phones in their pockets during class as long as the phones stay silent. This rule sounds fair, but it is not working. Westbrook should require students to keep phones in their lockers from the first bell until the last.

The main problem is distraction. When a phone buzzes or lights up, students glance at it, and their attention leaves the lesson. In a survey of 35 Westbrook teachers, 30 said phones interrupt their lessons at least once a day. Several teachers said they spend more time reminding students to put phones away than answering questions about the work.

A locker rule could also improve grades. Last year, Ashby Middle School tried a locker rule for one semester. Ashby reported that average math test scores rose by six points during that time.

The rule would help students connect with each other, too. My cousin goes to a school with a locker rule, and she says lunch is much more fun now because everyone talks instead of scrolling.

Some parents worry that they will not be able to reach their children in an emergency. This concern is reasonable. However, the front office already has a phone, and parents can call it at any time. Office staff can find any student within minutes.

Phones are useful tools, but they do not belong in the middle of a lesson. A locker rule would give Westbrook students a better chance to focus and learn.$q$, 'draft')
