   $q$Why is Elias Brandt's claim about iron bridges the weakest argument in the debate?$q$, $q$[{"text":"He supports it only by saying \"everyone knows,\" with no examples or facts.","is_correct":true},{"text":"He agrees with the supporters and then changes his mind at the end.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"},{"text":"He uses numbers from the ferry office that turn out to be incorrect.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"He gives too many examples, which makes his point confusing to follow.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$He supports it only by saying "everyone knows," with no examples or facts.$q$, $q$The passage says Brandt "offered no examples," so his claim rests on a vague appeal to what "everyone knows." The ferry office records were used by the supporters, not by Brandt.$q$, 'draft', '97bbbdaf-e60f-5c37-a446-17680f7cbadf')
