-- Passage rg7-argument-evidence-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('dfdebb9f-0616-5c80-8dec-1521c2f8d0a5', 'rg7-argument-evidence-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Shade Trees for Hollis Street$q$, $q$Hollis Street is the hottest street in the city of Brightwater. It has wide sidewalks, dark pavement and almost no trees. The city council should plant shade trees along its full length.

The strongest reason is heat. Last July, students from Brightwater Middle School measured sidewalk temperatures on two streets at 3 p.m. on five different days. On Hollis Street, the sidewalk averaged 128 degrees Fahrenheit. On nearby Elm Row, which is lined with large maples, the sidewalk averaged 94 degrees. The students used the same thermometer and measured at the same time each day.

Shade trees could also save money. When buildings stay cooler, people run their air conditioners less. A report from the city's energy office estimated that shade from grown trees could lower cooling costs for Hollis Street apartments by about 15 percent.

Trees bring other benefits as well. Their roots soak up rainwater, which reduces flooding. Many people also find that streets with trees are more pleasant for walking.

Some residents point out that trees cost money to plant and care for, and that roots can crack sidewalks. These are real costs. However, the city can choose smaller kinds of trees with gentle roots, and the energy savings would help balance the cost over time.

Planting trees will not cool Hollis Street overnight, since young trees take years to grow. But the sooner the city plants them, the sooner residents will feel the difference.$q$, 'draft')
