-- Passage rg7-argument-evidence-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7b3d51ac-2729-566d-8092-73d5770ad2eb', 'rg7-argument-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Quiz Yourself to Remember$q$, $q$Many students study for a test by rereading their notes again and again. It feels productive, because the material looks more familiar each time. However, students would remember more if they spent that time quizzing themselves instead.

When you quiz yourself, you have to pull information out of your memory without looking. This effort is what makes the memory stronger. Rereading, on the other hand, lets your eyes slide over words you already recognize, so your brain does very little work.

A study at a university learning center tested this idea with 80 students. All of them read the same article about volcanoes. Half then reread the article twice. The other half closed the article and wrote down everything they could remember, twice. One week later, both groups took the same test. The self-quiz group scored an average of 72 percent, while the rereading group scored 55 percent.

Teachers have noticed similar patterns. Mr. Adeyemi, a science teacher at Northgate Middle School, began starting each class with a three-question quiz on earlier lessons. He says his students now seem to remember old topics better by the end of the year.

Self-quizzing is easy to try. Students can cover their notes and explain a topic out loud, use flash cards or write practice questions for a friend.

Some students say quizzing themselves feels harder and more frustrating than rereading. That is true, but the difficulty is part of why it works. A little struggle while studying means less struggle on test day.$q$, 'draft')
