   $q$How does the speaker's view of the thin walls change from the beginning to the end of the poem?$q$, $q$[{"text":"They shift from a small problem to a reason to move away.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-change"},{"text":"They stay a problem that the speaker learns to put up with.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-change"},{"text":"They shift from a reason to hide to a way of making a friend.","is_correct":true},{"text":"They shift from useful to annoying once the knocking begins.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-change"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$They shift from a reason to hide to a way of making a friend.$q$, $q$At first the walls make the speaker play "small and gray," but by the end the speaker plays loudly "for the wall to hear" and visits Mrs. Abernathy. The walls become a connection, not something merely put up with.$q$, 'draft', '4b8624ce-4220-588f-a840-c59d8eaca327')
