   $q$Text 1 says the work was "slow," and Text 2 says the canal made shipping faster and cheaper. How can both be accurate?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Text 2 is mistaken, because a story written from experience is always more accurate.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misjudges-source"},{"text":"The boats moved slowly for a person walking beside them, yet they still beat costly wagons.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Text 1 describes the canal before 1825, when boats had no mules to pull them at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Text 1 describes boats on a lake, while Text 2 describes boats on the canal itself.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-detail"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The boats moved slowly for a person walking beside them, yet they still beat costly wagons.$q$, $q$Text 2 compares the canal with slow, expensive wagon travel, while Ezra feels the slowness of walking all day beside plodding mules. Text 1 is a story, not a firsthand record, so it does not overrule the factual account.$q$, 'draft', '425533a7-f548-511c-b3d7-b5d543092bb1')
