-- Passage rg7-comparing-texts-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1a0e79f8-f430-5083-b43a-df142f3577e1', 'rg7-comparing-texts-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$When the Dust Rolled In$q$, $q$Text 1: Nell's Notebook (a story told in diary entries)

April 3, 1935. Another dust storm today. It came in the afternoon like a brown wall rolling across the fields. Mama hung wet sheets over the windows, and we tied damp cloths over our noses. Even so, there was grit in the bread at supper.

April 9. Our neighbors, the Kowalskis, packed their truck and drove west this morning. Mr. Kowalski said there was nothing left to farm. I gave Rosie my blue ribbon so she would remember me. The house feels quieter now, even with the wind.

May 20. A man from the government came to talk with Papa. He showed him how to plow in curving rows that follow the shape of the land, so the rain soaks in instead of running off. He said we should plant a row of trees along the north field to slow the wind. Papa listened for a long time. Tonight he said, "Maybe this land can heal."

Text 2: The Dust Bowl (a factual account)

During the 1930s, a severe drought struck the southern Great Plains of the United States. For years before, farmers had plowed up the deep-rooted native grasses that held the soil in place. When the rain stopped, the dry, loose soil blew away.

Powerful winds lifted the soil into huge dust clouds, sometimes called "black blizzards." Families hung wet sheets over doors and windows and covered their faces with cloth. Thousands of families left the region, many heading west to look for work.

The government responded with programs to protect the soil. Farmers learned contour plowing, which means plowing along the curves of the land to hold water. Workers also planted long rows of trees, called shelterbelts, to block the wind.$q$, 'draft')
