-- Passage rg7-connections-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('68c24f9b-c2a7-5495-b00a-1d18351220e2', 'rg7-connections-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How the Refrigerator Changed the Kitchen$q$, $q$Before electric refrigerators, keeping food cold was hard work. Many families used an icebox, a wooden cabinet lined with metal. A block of ice sat in the top section, and cold air sank down over the food. Every few days, a delivery worker brought a new block of ice, because the old one melted.

Because food spoiled quickly, families shopped almost every day. Milk, meat, and fresh vegetables had to be eaten soon after they were bought. Many people also preserved food by salting, drying, or canning it so it would last through the winter.

Electric refrigerators became common in homes during the 1930s and 1940s. They used a liquid that absorbed heat as it turned into a gas, carrying warmth out of the box. Families no longer needed ice deliveries, and food stayed fresh much longer.

This new machine changed more than the kitchen. Since food lasted for a week or more, people could shop less often and buy more at a time. Large grocery stores with wide aisles began to replace many small neighborhood shops. Food companies started selling frozen vegetables and other items that only made sense if customers had a freezer at home.

Farming changed as well. Refrigerated trucks and train cars could carry fruit and vegetables across the country without spoiling. A family in a cold northern city could buy fresh lettuce in January that had been grown hundreds of miles away.

Today, most people rarely think about their refrigerator. Yet this quiet box helped reshape how people shop, eat, and get their food.$q$, 'draft')
