insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8ecb03e1-6891-583a-a797-3ac053ef04fc', 'rg7-context-clues-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Nature's Engineers$q$, $q$Beavers are sometimes called nature's engineers. Using only their teeth, paws, and the materials around them, they can change the shape of an entire stream.

A beaver begins by finding a spot where water flows slowly. It will gnaw through the trunks of small trees, chewing steadily with its sharp front teeth until the trees fall. Then it drags the branches into the water and piles them up with mud and stones to form a dam.

Behind the dam, the water rises and spreads out into a pond. In the middle of the pond, the beaver builds its lodge. This is not a hotel or a cabin, but a dome-shaped home made of sticks and mud. The entrance to the lodge is submerged, completely underwater, so predators such as coyotes cannot easily get inside.

Beaver ponds benefit many other living things. Frogs, ducks, and fish thrive in the calm water, growing and multiplying in ways they could not in a fast stream. Insects become abundant, and birds that eat insects arrive to feed on the plentiful supply.

Not everyone welcomes beavers, however. A dam can flood a road or a farmer's field. In some places, people install pipes through dams to lower the water level. This approach lets the beavers stay while reducing the damage. It is a compromise that gives both people and animals part of what they need.$q$, 'draft')
