-- Passage rg7-evidence-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0f15e3de-08ef-5e31-bf53-4231f29ae673', 'rg7-evidence-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Nature's Engineers$q$, $q$Beavers are sometimes called nature's engineers. With strong front teeth and steady effort, a family of beavers can cut down small trees and build a dam across a stream. The dam slows the water and creates a pond. In that pond, the beavers build a lodge, a dome of sticks and mud. Its underwater entrances help keep predators out.

For many years, people treated beaver dams mostly as a nuisance. Dams can flood roads and farm fields, and beavers chew through trees that landowners want to keep. In many areas, beavers were trapped or removed.

Scientists now view beaver ponds in a more complicated way. A pond spreads water across a wide area instead of letting it rush downstream. Some of that water soaks into the ground and refills underground supplies. During dry summers, streams below beaver dams often keep flowing longer than streams without them.

The ponds also become homes for other creatures. Frogs lay eggs in the calm water. Ducks and herons feed along the edges. Young fish can hide among the drowned branches. In one study area, researchers counted more kinds of birds near beaver ponds than along nearby stretches of stream without dams.

Some communities have started working with beavers instead of against them. Workers install pipes through dams so that pond levels stay low enough to protect roads. Others wrap the trunks of valuable trees with wire. These methods let the beavers stay while limiting the damage.

Beavers have not changed their behavior. What has changed is how people understand the effects of that behavior.$q$, 'draft')
