-- Passage rg7-evidence-inference-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b282c102-d8f5-538b-9475-b9d1ae229035', 'rg7-evidence-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Riding in Someone's Shadow$q$, $q$Watch a long-distance bicycle race, and you will notice that riders rarely spread out across the road. Instead, they line up close behind one another or ride in a large pack. This habit, called drafting, is one of the most important tactics in the sport.

Drafting works because of air resistance. A cyclist moving at racing speed must push a large amount of air out of the way. The rider at the front does most of that work. Riders tucked behind face less air pushing against them, so they can keep the same speed while pedaling with less effort. Studies in wind tunnels have found that a rider in the middle of a pack may need far less energy than a rider alone.

Because the front position is so tiring, teammates take turns. One rider leads for a short stretch, then moves aside and drops to the back of the line. The next rider takes over. By sharing the hardest job, the whole group can travel faster than any single rider could manage alone.

Drafting also shapes race strategy. A team may protect its strongest rider by keeping her sheltered for most of the race. Near the finish, teammates who have done the hard work at the front peel away. The sheltered rider then sprints for the line with the energy she has saved.

Drafting has a few risks. Riders must stay only inches apart, so a sudden brake or a wobble can cause a crash. Riders in a pack also have a harder time seeing the road ahead. For these reasons, young cyclists usually practice drafting slowly with a coach before trying it in a race.$q$, 'draft')
